hidclass: let xinput drive a pad that keeps its own identity.

wine's xinput finds its devices by enumerating one private interface, and until
now only winexinput's &XI_ child carried it. Reaching that child costs the pad
everything that makes it itself: winexinput claims the devnode, and what the
rest of the system is offered instead is an Xbox-shaped, input-only device --
no adaptive triggers, no touchpad, no PlayStation glyphs, and Sony's own
library cannot open it at all.

None of that is necessary. The interface is only how xinput FINDS a device; it
opens whatever it finds and drives it through the haptics collection. So a
gamepad that has one is offered under that interface as well as its own,
exactly the way this file already offers a mouse and a keyboard under theirs.
The device is not replaced, wrapped or hidden: it keeps GUID_DEVINTERFACE_HID
and every client that reads it today reads the same bytes tomorrow.

The condition is not a list of product ids but a question about the descriptor
-- does it carry a haptics collection -- which is exactly "can xinput rumble
this". Devices that already carry the private class, winexinput's own children,
are left alone, so an Xbox pad behaves as before.

Signed-off-by: MacGameVideoFix
